      <p class="muted">
        <strong>This is a short summary.</strong> The full note, with its photo plates, timings and references, is
        under review at <em>Indian BIRDS</em>. Journals ask for work that has not been published before, so I am
        keeping the full text and the plates back until the editors decide. The pictures on this page are other frames
        from the same videos.
      </p>

      <h2>The film: <em>Season: Out of Tune</em></h2>
      <p>
        These sap observations are one chapter of <em>Season: Out of Tune</em>, a film I shot and narrated over the
        same summer. It follows one hot season on the fringes of Mukundara: Indian grey wolves cooling off in a
        shrinking wetland, Bengal monitors wrestling, Sarus Cranes dancing, a chinkara looking for water, and the pitta
        pair raising chicks through the heatwave while waiting for the monsoon. The Arjuna and Indian ash sap come in
        around the eight-minute mark.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where it was filmed.</strong> The film was not shot inside the tiger reserve's forest. It was shot on
        the fringes, among the villages, farmland, waterbodies and grasslands that sit around Mukundara Hills Tiger
        Reserve in Kota district. These are working lands, shared by farmers, herders and wildlife. When I say
        "Mukundara" here I mean that wider landscape, not the reserve.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/field-reports/season-out-of-tune/">Watch the film, with its chapters</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>A summer out of tune</h2>
      <p>
        It was hard to spend that summer in the field and not think about the heat. By late May the heat was relentless. Kota touched 46.4&nbsp;°C on 28 May, the hottest May day there in eight
        years, and the wetlands were shrinking by the week. A <a href="/field-reports/raksha-lone-indian-grey-wolf-world-environment-day/">lone wolf</a> was resting in the shallow water at Alania. Then a western disturbance broke the heat on 29 May,
        and the Arjuna went quiet.
      </p>
      <p>
        I can't say the heat caused anything I saw at the sap. One summer of notes can't show that. What I can say is
        that this summer was hotter and longer than the seasons allow, and that the wildlife and the people living on
        these fringes had to get through it with whatever they had. That, more than any single observation, is what the film is about.
      </p>
