      <h2>What I watched</h2>
      <p>
        The trees are one Arjuna (<em>Terminalia arjuna</em>) and two Indian ash (<em>Lannea coromandelica</em>),
        all within about a kilometre and a half of each other in the dry deciduous forest of the Mukundara Hills
        landscape. Nobody had cut the bark to make them bleed. The sap was coming out on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        I watched everything from my car, parked on a village trail that local bikes, cars and tractors use every day,
        20 to 50 metres from the trees. I used binoculars and a 600 mm lens and never got out. Each visit is logged as
        an eBird checklist.
      </p>

      <h2>Birds at the Arjuna</h2>
      <p>
        Between 12 and 29 May the Arjuna had one small opening, about the width of a thumb, and a steady line of
        visitors fed at it: Brown-capped Pygmy Woodpecker, Black-rumped Flameback, Cinereous Tit, Indian White-eye,
        Common Tailorbird and a palm squirrel. From where I sat I could not tell whether the small insect-eaters were
        taking the sap itself or tiny insects drawn to it, so I only say they fed at the opening.
      </p>
      <p>
        On 12 May a pygmy woodpecker sat next to the wound for a long time without feeding. A flameback came, pecked
        it open and fed, and once it left the pygmy went straight in. Maybe the bigger bird made the sap reachable.
        I can't say more than that from one morning.
      </p>
