          <figcaption>Brown-capped Pygmy Woodpecker at the opening, 19 May 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        The last feeding I saw was on 29 May, the day the heatwave broke. I passed the tree on eighteen more visits
        through June and never saw anything there again.
      </p>

      <h2>Langurs and pittas at the Indian ash</h2>
      <p>
        At the two Indian ash trees, langurs fed on the sap, which is golden-brown when fresh and nearly black when it
        dries. One picked a lump off the trunk and ate it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On one of these trees a line of dried sap ran down the trunk and pooled on the ground. On three mornings at
        the end of June and start of July I watched Indian Pittas crouched on that patch with their feathers spread,
        the way birds sit when they are anting. There were ants around the flow, but at that distance I could not see
        them on the birds, so I am not sure whether the pittas were after the sap, the ants, or both. The birds were
        nesting nearby. I never went near the nest.
      </p>
      <div class="sap-photo-grid">
        <figure class="evidence-card field-photo-card">
          <a href="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-ash-dark-exudate-trunk-1600.jpg"><img src="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-ash-dark-exudate-trunk-1600.jpg" alt="Dark, glossy exudate running down the cracked bark of an Indian ash trunk" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a>
          <figcaption>Exudate on an Indian ash trunk, 2 July 2026. It dries almost black.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="evidence-card field-photo-card">
          <a href="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-ash-langur-at-trunk-1600.jpg"><img src="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-ash-langur-at-trunk-1600.jpg" alt="Langur at the base of an Indian ash trunk with its face against the bark" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a>
          <figcaption>A langur at the trunk of the ash, 1 July 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="evidence-card field-photo-card">
          <a href="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-pitta-pair-indian-ash-1600.jpg"><img src="/assets/imgs/field-reports/tree-sap-indian-pitta-pair-indian-ash-1600.jpg" alt="A pair of Indian Pittas on the ground at the base of an Indian ash tree in the Mukundara Hills landscape" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a>
          <figcaption>The pitta pair at the foot of the ash, 1 July 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>
